  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increasing shower and thunderstorm chances\nSunday through early next week as low pressure and a series of \nfronts cross the region.\n\nBack toward the typical summertime regime Sunday into the first half \nof next week with daily chances for showers and thunderstorms. The \ngreatest coverage of showers and thunderstorms appears to be Sunday \ninto Monday as a potent upper level trough ejects eastward from the \nGreat Lakes into the Ohio River Valley. The incumbent upper level \ntrough looks to be pinched off as it dives south and east yielding a \ncut low pressure system which looks to cross the area late Sunday \ninto Monday. The cutoff low and associated cold front will bring \nwidespread shower and thunderstorm chances to the region both days \nalong with abundant cloud cover. Shear and instability values look \nto remain low due to weak winds aloft and widespread cloudiness. \nEven with that said, GoogleWxNext2 and NSSL probabilities have 10 to \n30 percent contours over the region during the Sunday and Monday \ntimeframe. This will be dictated by the placement and timing of the \nlow/associated front as it crosses the region. Moisture will be \nample ahead of the boundary on southerly flow although instability \nwill be lacking with modeled CAPE values less than a few hundred \nj/kg given the extra cloud cover and timing of the system beyond the \npeak heating window. \n\nBeneficial rainfall is likely given the coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms. Overall looking at half an inch to one inch of rain \nSunday through the middle of next week. Locally higher totals are \npossible under heavier thunderstorms and across portions of the \nAlleghenies/Shenandoah Valley where the low looks to track through. \nThe flooding threat remains lo, but not zero given repetitive rounds \nof thunderstorms (especially over urban/poor drainage areas). \n\nBeyond Monday, diurnal chances for showers and thunderstorms look to \ncontinue as the front stalls near or just south of the area. The \nnext cold front and upper level trough will follow with another \nround of widespread thunderstorm chances later in the workweek. High \ntemperatures will remain at or just above normal through the period. \nSunday and Monday look to be the coolest with highs in the upper 70s \nand mid 80s. Highs get back close to 90 Tuesday through Thursday \nnext week. Overnight lows will fall back into the mid to upper\n60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Primarily dry conditions through Saturday.\n\nUpper level low pressure is spinning over southern New England\ntoday, while a surface low moves off Cape Cod. The gradient\nbetween this low and high pressure in the Great Lakes/Ohio\nValley is resulting in another day of gusty northwest winds.\nWater vapor imagery is showing deeper moisture with the upper \nlow is pulling to the east. Even so, some models still show \nanother spoke of energy rotating southward this afternoon and \nevening, perhaps producing a few showers across northeastern \nMaryland. The current forecast is dry, and am not convinced \nthere is a high enough chance of rain to add it to the forecast.\nRH forecasts do suggest cumulus or altocumulus clouds may \nincrease across the northeastern quadrant of the CWA this \nafternoon. Temperatures will remain seasonable or slightly below\nnormal through tonight, when lighter winds may allow rural \nareas to decouple. Highs this afternoon are generally in the \n80s, with lows tonight from the mid 50s to mid 60s (perhaps\nlocally even cooler in the higher elevations).\n\nThe surface high will reach the area Friday. Shortwave ridging\nwill approach from the west as the upper low departs New England.\nDry conditions are expected with high temperatures closer to \nclimatology in the mid 80s to near 90 and lows in the 60s to \naround 70.\n\nTemperatures may warm a couple more degrees Saturday as the high\nmoves off the coast and increasing southerly flow develops.\nClouds will likely start increasing as low pressure at the\nsurface and aloft moves into the Ohio Valley. There is some\nuncertainty with how quickly rain chances arrive. Some showers\ncould reach the Alleghenies as early as Saturday afternoon, but\nrecent trends are suggesting it will be delayed until Saturday \nnight. Much of the area, especially east of the Blue Ridge, may \nremain dry until Sunday.",
